---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const jsonLd = [{ '@type': 'WebPage', name: 'Privacy', description: 'How Nopload keeps your images on your device.' }];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy: Your Images Never Leave Your Device | Nopload"
  description="Nopload has no server that receives images, no analytics and no cookies. Here is how it works and how you can check it yourself in DevTools."
  path="/privacy"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="prose mx-auto max-w-2xl space-y-6">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted">Short version: your images never leave your device. There is nothing to upload them to.</p>
    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">What happens to your images</h2>
      <p>
        When you add an image, your browser reads it into memory. All processing (background removal, compression, conversion, resizing,
        metadata removal) runs in this tab, in WebAssembly and on your GPU when available. Results are created in the tab and saved by your
        browser's normal download. Closing the tab discards everything.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">What we collect</h2>
      <p>Nothing. No analytics, no cookies, no fingerprinting, no third-party scripts, fonts or CDNs. There is no account to create.</p>
      <p>
        Your theme choice is kept in your browser's local storage, and the app files (and the AI model, if you choose to download it) are kept
        in your browser's cache so the app works offline. You can clear both from your browser settings.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">How it is enforced</h2>
      <p>
        Every page is served with a Content-Security-Policy that only allows connections to this site (<code>connect-src 'self'</code>). Even if
        some code tried to send data elsewhere, the browser would block it.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Check it yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Click the <strong>0 uploads</strong> badge at the top of any page to see every request the page has made. Or open DevTools, go to the
        Network tab, process an image and watch: no request carries your file.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The site is static files on Cloudflare Pages. Like any web host, Cloudflare may log standard request data (such as IP address and
        page URL) to deliver and protect the site. Your images are never part of any request.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
